-- MySQL Predefined Databases
1. mysql Database(Administration Office) - It stores information about MySQL itself
Examples include: User accounts,Password authentication,Roles, Privileges/permissions, Server configuration metadata

2.information_schema(MySQL's Dictionary) - it stores metadata(Metadata is data about/inside another data)
Example: Suppose you create a table anukundham
CREATE TABLE users (user_id INT, name VARCHAR(50) ); --The actual user data will go into your users table

But where does MySQL remember things like:
->Table name
->Column names
->Data types
-> Number of columns -> That's metadata And it's available through the information_schema

              MySQL Sever->DB's Data ->Table's Data -> rows&Columns-> values

3. Performance_schema(Dashboard) -  which monitors MySQL insights 
It collects performance-related information such as:
- Query execution times
- Wait events
- Resource usage
- Connection activity -> DBAs use it to identify bottlenecks and troubleshoot slow systems(High execution Time)

4. sys(Whole system reports) -"If we already have performance_schema, why do we need sys???" enduku
The performance_schema contains a lot of detailed information, but it can be difficult to read directly
--> The sys database provides simplified views and reports built on top of performance_schema
==> You can think sys as a dashboard that presents performance information in a more user-friendly way

 Database                          Purpose                                    Used By                    
 mysql              | User accounts, privileges, internal configuration  | DBAs and MySQL             
 information_schema | Metadata about databases, tables, columns, indexes | Developers & DBAs          
 performance_schema | Server performance monitoring                      | DBAs                       
 sys                | Simplified performance reports and views           | DBAs & Advanced Developers 

